0]_#_[#cc0ee]","[#658e0]_#_[#e7b81]","[#cc0ee]_#_[#e7b81]","[#53edc]_#_[#01d3b]","[#53edc]_#_[#0f47c]","[#0f47c]_#_[#01d3b]"],"description":"Each eye cell first conditions our signals to just see a rising edge, using simple Feed Forward Inhibition. This means if you continue to stimulate each input, we only see a single spike when we first see the input activate.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775001653547","neuronKeys":["[#e7b81]","[#bac40]","[#d06c7]"],"edgeStrings":["[#e7b81]_#_[#bac40]","[#e7b81]_#_[#d06c7]"],"description":"Each conditioned output will stimulate two different inhibitory cells to help make a comparison. One is delayed, the other fires right away.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775001714803","neuronKeys":["[#01d3b]","[#2487e]","[#98ee3]"],"edgeStrings":["[#01d3b]_#_[#98ee3]","[#01d3b]_#_[#2487e]"],"description":"The input sends a very strong signal to the two inhibitory output cells, so that we ensure they activate with a single spike!","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775001793123","neuronKeys":["[#bac40]","[#66739]","[#26de2]","[#4289c]","[#2487e]"],"edgeStrings":["[#bac40]_#_[#66739]","[#2487e]_#_[#4289c]","[#4289c]_#_[#26de2]","[#66739]_#_[#26de2]"],"description":"This group of 5 neurons acts like a logical ‘AND’ gate.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775001836579","neuronKeys":["[#26de2]","[#3510a]"],"edgeStrings":[],"description":"These are the outputs of our two AND gates. They are excitatory neurons that are set to be highly active. They want to fire all the time even without any input.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775001927399","neuronKeys":["[#4289c]","[#66739]"],"edgeStrings":["[#66739]_#_[#26de2]","[#4289c]_#_[#26de2]"],"description":"Working backwards, these two inhibitory neurons are also highly active and set to fire without the need for inputs. Their output is mild because a small ammount of inhibition can stop the excitatory neuron from firing. When nothing is happening, these will keep the output excitatory neuron quiet.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002031969","neuronKeys":["[#bac40]","[#4289c]","[#66739]"],"edgeStrings":["[#bac40]_#_[#66739]","[#4289c]_#_[#26de2]"],"description":"If only one of the two inputs to our AND gate are triggered, the other inhibitory neuron will still keep the output from firing.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002092285","neuronKeys":["[#66739]","[#bac40]","[#4289c]","[#2487e]","[#26de2]"],"edgeStrings":["[#bac40]_#_[#66739]","[#2487e]_#_[#4289c]"],"description":"If both input inhibitory neurons fire, at the same time, it can allow the output excitatory neuron to fire briefly!","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002175567","neuronKeys":["[#26de2]","[#2487e]","[#bac40]"],"edgeStrings":["[#a2dd3]_#_[#658e0]","[#a2dd3]_#_[#53edc]"],"description":"If we receive the delayed signal from one input at the same time as a fresh input from the other zone, we know an object must be moving in a certain direction!","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002240407","neuronKeys":["[#164da]","[#40b46]","[#26de2]"],"edgeStrings":["[#26de2]_#_[#164da]","[#164da]_#_[#40b46]","[#40b46]_#_[#164da]"],"description":"When we sense an object moving in one direction, we can then stimulate a Recurrent Pair of neurons which will oscillate indefinitely and remember which direction an object traveled across Spikerbot’s vision.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002291045","neuronKeys":["[#26de2]","[#e6c76]"],"edgeStrings":["[#26de2]_#_[#e6c76]","[#e6c76]_#_[#2916e]","[#e6c76]_#_[#742ef]"],"description":"We also will supress the memory of an object moving in the opposite direction. The most recent direction will persist.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002334642","neuronKeys":["[#40b46]","[#742ef]"],"edgeStrings":["[#40b46]_#_[#04ddd]","[#742ef]_#_[#04ddd]"],"description":"Each pair will blink the left lights yellow, or the rightside lights red to show you which direction an object was last seen moving.","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002403604","neuronKeys":["[#658e0]","[#53edc]"],"edgeStrings":[],"description":"You can experiment with changing the inputs. Any two signals can be compared this way to detect a time delay event! Try detecting low and high pitch from the microphone to detect an upward or downward tone whistle!","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002553523","neuronKeys":["[#40b46]","[#742ef]"],"edgeStrings":[],"description":"Similarly the outputs can control any command. If tracking an object, the memory of which way an object last went could be useful if we loose sight of it!","thumbnail":""},{"id":"1775002830180","neuronKeys":["[#2487e]","[#d06c7]"],"edgeStrings":[],"description":"You can also change the delay time, to compare events that happen fast one after another or slow. If the delays are too short, Spikerbot may be confused as to which direction something is moving.","thumbnail":""}],"info":{"title":"Elementary Motion Detection","description":"Replicate a fruitfly’s ability to detect the direction of an object moving at a set speed! This functions similarly to networks that are downstream compound eye cells just at or before the Lobula Plate in the fly brain’s optical lobe.","author":"User","version":"1.0.1","timestamp":"2026-04-08T15:43:35.035224"}}